# Copy to `.env.local` for local development. On Vercel these are registered as
# Project Environment Variables, not committed files.
#
# See ADR-0014 (docs/decisions/adr-0014-web-session-handling.md), ADR-0035 (the compose
# `web` service) and the contract at docs/contracts/web-api-client.md.
#
# `docker compose up` needs nothing from this file: `docker-compose.yml`'s `web` service
# sets API_BASE_URL itself and deliberately sets no BFF_PROXY_SECRET. Neither of the two
# variables the API's own boot reads — BETTER_AUTH_SECRET, DATABASE_AUTH_URL — belongs
# here; this app is a Vercel project environment and reads no database DSN and no signing
# key (TASK-009, F-036).

# THERE IS NO HOSTED API. No deploy target is chosen for `apps/api` (ADR-0030) and
# nothing is running at a public hostname; `shortkit-api.fly.dev` never existed and
# both values below used to point at it. The only API a value here can reach is the
# one in the local compose stack, and the two variables now have DIFFERENT values
# because they are read from different places.
#
# Browser-visible. Never used for anything authenticated — the browser only ever
# calls this same-origin app, never the API directly (ADR-0014). Must include the
# API's global prefix: ADR-0006 sets it to `/api`, and the redirect controller is
# registered outside that prefix, so a base URL missing `/api` reaches the redirect
# surface instead of the JSON one.
#
# `localhost:3001` is the API's port as `docker-compose.yml` publishes it, so this
# is reachable from a browser on the machine running the stack and nowhere else. It
# is NOT `api:3001`: that name resolves only on the compose network, and no browser
# can resolve it.
#
# This value is genuinely public, so `assert:no-secrets`
# (apps/web/scripts/assert-no-inlined-secrets.mjs) does not treat it as a leak
# target (F-154, ruled by Juano). It is that script's positive control: once some
# non-test file under apps/web reads `process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL`, the
# check asserts its value appears somewhere under .next, proving the build ran with
# real values rather than nothing (F-156/F-161/F-167). Nothing sets it for the
# compose build, deliberately — Next inlines it at build time and any value baked in
# would be wrong on some other machine (ADR-0035).
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3001/api

# Server-only, and read at REQUEST time rather than inlined, so it is the compose
# network's name for the API rather than a published port. Never prefix this (or
# BFF_PROXY_SECRET below) with NEXT_PUBLIC_ — that prefix inlines the value into the
# client bundle, which is exactly what `assert:no-secrets` guards against for
# BFF_PROXY_SECRET. It must include `/api` (ADR-0006). Not confidential, so it is
# not itself searched for by `assert:no-secrets`.
#
# READ, AND REQUIRED AT REQUEST TIME. The BFF proxy route,
# apps/web/app/api/bff/[...path]/route.ts (TASK-007), forwards every `apiClient()`
# call to it, and the session module (src/lib/session/session.ts) mints access tokens
# through it. Both throw on an unset value rather than defaulting, so a missing
# variable is a 500 on the first proxied request, not a silent wrong host. The
# route builds `/api/<path>` against this base by replacing the path, so the `/api`
# suffix is not doubled.
#
# The value below is the compose stack's, where `docker-compose.yml` sets the same
# literal for the `web` service. Running `next dev` on the host against that stack
# is the one case where this differs: there the API is on the published port, so
# `.env.local` gets API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3001/api instead.
API_BASE_URL=http://api:3001/api

# Server-only. Shared with the API, wherever it runs. The API's constant-time match on this
# value is what it trusts before honouring a forwarded client address
# (`x-shortkit-client-ip`); published to the browser it would let anyone forge that
# header and collapse every IP-keyed rate limit into one shared bucket. Never
# logged on the Vercel side.
#
# OPTIONAL FOR A LOCAL STACK, and unset in `docker-compose.yml` on purpose. When it is
# unset the BFF route forwards NEITHER `x-shortkit-client-ip` NOR
# `x-shortkit-proxy-auth`, and the API — which declares no BFF_TRUST_BOUNDARY there —
# asks for no secret, so nothing refuses and nothing mismatches. What that costs is
# stated in ADR-0040: no IP-keyed rate bucket binds in that stack. Setting it here
# means setting the SAME value on the API side with BFF_TRUST_BOUNDARY=bff
# (apps/api/.env.example); a value on one side only is counted as a mismatch there
# and honoured nowhere.
#
# Required format: base64url alphabet only — A-Z, a-z, 0-9, "-", "_" — no "+", "/"
# or "=" padding, at least 32 characters. `assert:no-secrets` enforces both the
# length and the charset (F-160, F-164, F-165): a value outside this alphabet or
# shorter than 32 characters fails the check outright, before it can be inlined
# anywhere. The charset restriction is deliberate, not incidental — no character
# in it is ever rendered in an HTML-entity, unicode-escape or JSON-escaped form,
# which is what lets a plain substring search stand in for chasing every possible
# escaping scheme.
#
# Generate with:
#   openssl rand 24 | base64 | tr '+/' '-_' | tr -d '='
# 24 bytes of entropy, encoded as exactly 32 base64url characters with no
# padding — the floor this check enforces, not a target to trim toward.
BFF_PROXY_SECRET=
